course: dictionary
lesson: 10537
title: Ulcer and body words in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words come from health and science, and you may meet them in a text before you have heard them at home. Context helps here too. Look at the words around the new word. «Hand, foot and mouth disease is a mild childhood virus with mouth ulcers and spots on the hands and feet.» The words spots, virus and disease tell you this is about illness.
explain: The word mouth before ulcers is a clue. An ulcer is a sore place where the surface has broken down. It is slow to heal and can hurt. A mouth ulcer is a small, painful white or yellow spot inside your mouth.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Do mouth ulcers fit with spots on the hands and feet? Yes, both are sore marks on the body. The last clue, «unrelated to the cattle disease», tells you the illness has nothing to do with cows, so do not mix the two up.
fact: An ulcer is a sore place on the skin or inside the body where the surface has broken down.
fact: A mouth ulcer is a small, painful white or yellow spot inside your mouth.
fact: Mouth ulcers are common and usually harmless.
fact: A stomach ulcer is a sore in the lining of the stomach, and it needs a doctor.
fact: «Ulcer» is a noun, and its plural is «ulcers».
exercise: «Hand, foot and mouth disease is a mild childhood virus with mouth ulcers and spots on the hands and feet.» What is a mouth ulcer? | A loud noise made with the mouth | A kind of cattle disease | *A small, painful white or yellow spot inside your mouth | A tasty sweet you suck slowly
exercise: «Hand, foot and mouth disease is a mild childhood virus with mouth ulcers and spots on the hands and feet.» Which word tells you the illness mostly affects young children? | *childhood | spots | unrelated | virus
exercise: Which fact about a stomach ulcer is true? | It is a type of sweet | It is a small white spot on the tongue | *It is a sore in the lining of the stomach and needs a doctor | It is a kind of cattle disease
test: Mouth ulcers are common and harmless. What does harmless mean? | Dangerous and likely to hurt you badly | *Not dangerous, and not likely to cause serious harm | Hard to find | Very long and thin
test: «...is a mild childhood virus..., unrelated to the cattle disease.» What does unrelated mean here? | *It has nothing to do with the other thing | It is closely connected to the cattle disease | It only happens to cows | It is a type of mouth ulcer
exam: Which word in «a mild childhood virus» tells you the illness is not serious? | disease | childhood | *mild | virus
